# Ocentra Parent Domain Boundaries

Current architecture authority is Rust-first. New work must treat Rust as the
product source of truth even when older plan docs still describe the former
TypeScript-first migration path.

Canonical cross-boundary schema truth lives in `crates/schema`. Rust domain and
runtime crates own product contracts, actions, route snapshots, read models,
projections, business logic, and policy decisions. TypeScript is presentation
and edge-adapter code only: TSX, CSS, assets, generated bridge DTO/types, thin
host/dev adapters, and minimal local visual state.

`@ocentra-parent/schema-domain`, `@ocentra-parent/agent-protocol-domain`, and
feature TS domain packages are transitional compatibility surfaces only. They
may keep temporary edge decoders or presentation helpers until Rust-owned
replacements and generated bridge consumers are live. Do not add new
hand-written TS business schema or TS-owned product contracts there.

## TypeScript Rules

- Use Effect Schema only at untrusted TypeScript edges or generated validation
  edges. It is not business truth.
- Do not add Zod.
- Do not create new hand-written product brands or DTO authorities in
  TypeScript.
- Keep external input as `unknown` until parsed.
- Do not place runtime route ids, event names, field names, protocol strings,
  endpoint paths, read-model rules, policy logic, or product fallback behavior
  in app or package source.
- TypeScript may render generated Rust-owned snapshots and dispatch generated
  Rust-owned actions. It may not reconstruct product state as source of truth.

## Package Ownership

| Package | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `crates/schema` | Canonical cross-boundary Rust DTOs, route snapshots, action/result shapes, generated TypeScript bridge artifacts, and encoded-shape tests. |
| `crates/parent-runtime-core` | Parent app facade: accepts UI actions, dispatches Rust commands/events, and builds route snapshots. |
| `crates/ocentra-eventing` | Internal Rust event backbone and business event decoupling. |
| Rust domain crates such as tracking, network, browser, policy, screen, app-game, enforcement, family, evidence, custody, notification | Product logic, projections, read models, security decisions, and runtime state for their domain. |
| `apps/parent-desktop/src-tauri` | Product Tauri command/event host into Rust. |
| `apps/portal` / future `apps/parent-ui` | Presentation only: TSX, CSS, assets, generated bridge DTO imports, host/dev adapters, and visual state. |
| `@ocentra-parent/logging-domain` | Narrow dev/proof/UI-edge logging helpers only; Rust owns product proof logs. |

Transitional TypeScript packages:

- `@ocentra-parent/schema-domain` must stop being canonical authority. Keep only
  temporary edge decoders or generated validation adapters until consumers move
  to Rust-owned generated DTOs.
- `@ocentra-parent/agent-protocol-domain` must stop being protocol authority.
  Rust parent/child crates consume `crates/schema` and Rust protocol shapes.
- Feature TS domain packages must shrink to pure presentation helpers or be
  removed after Rust replacement is live, the bridge exposes it, the UI consumes
  it, focused tests pass, and the main lane approves deletion.

## Rust Rules

- Runtime strings, Serde structs/enums, action payloads, route snapshots, read
  models, and wire DTOs that cross product boundaries live in `crates/schema` or
  the owning Rust domain crate when the shape is crate-local.
- `crates/agent-protocol` is transitional protocol surface, not the desired
  final schema owner. Prefer new shared cross-boundary DTOs in `crates/schema`.
- Rust services and runtimes consume Rust-owned constants and structs, not local
  string literals or TS-owned catalogs.
- Platform-neutral concepts belong outside Windows-specific modules.
- Generated TypeScript artifacts must preserve Rust serde encoded shape: field
  names, discriminants, nullability, versioning, and enum values.
- Shared Rust/generated-TS contracts must be guarded by serialization,
  generated-artifact, compatibility, and drift-check coverage so TypeScript
  cannot silently become a second authority.

## Change Order

1. Add or update the canonical Rust contract/read model/action in
   `crates/schema`, `crates/parent-runtime-core`, or the relevant Rust domain
   crate.
2. Add or update Rust serialization, round-trip, negative, compatibility, and
   drift coverage appropriate to the boundary.
3. Generate or update TypeScript bridge DTOs or edge decoders from Rust.
4. Update TS consumers to render generated snapshots or dispatch generated
   actions only.
5. Remove product TS fallback/reconstruction only after replacement is live and
   focused tests pass.
6. Delete/shrink TS business packages only after imports are gone, replacement
   tests are green, and the main lane approves the deletion condition.
7. Run scoped crate/package validation first; reserve broad repo validation for
   late integration.

## Rust Test Shape

Rust test placement must be visible and risk-based. Use crate-level folders such
as `tests/unit`, `tests/integration`, `tests/contract`, `tests/security`,
`tests/property`, `tests/fuzz`, `tests/replay`, `tests/concurrency`,
`tests/migration`, `tests/compatibility`, `tests/observability`,
`tests/performance`, `tests/chaos`, `tests/ai`, or `tests/e2e` only when that
category has real tests for that crate. Do not keep empty `.gitkeep` folders as
proof. New tests must live in visible crate-level test folders/groups. Treat
inline `#[cfg(test)]` blocks as tiny private invariant seams or migration debt
only; they do not count as closure evidence and must not be used for new
completion claims.
